Jim Miller wrote:

> The science of keymaking hasn't gotten very far in
> the dungeon, when every key opens every door.  It
> makes having a lock senseless.

Well, maybe, but earlier versions of NetHack (or
perhaps Hack) had different key types and locks only
openable by those keys. The result was so obnoxious
to play that the current system replaced it by
popular acclaim. Now, scarcity of keys is the most
important factor.

Thus, in the fact, DoD key technlogy has gone from a
highly scientific craft to an engineering matter, by
the use of standardized keys as commodities. It
isn't that the DoD locksmiths are unskilled, just
immensely stupid. Probably they each graduated at
the absolute bottoms of their classes at Gnome
School.

YANI 1: I do feel that the current game could use a
"cheap plastic fake key" that was much more likely
to be found than real keys, though, just to make key
finding less of a "mortal lock" on opening remaining
locks. To keep the game afoot, it should be the case
that those keys _as a type_ remain unidentifiable
and unnameable, until they break in use, or the
individual key is identified, so that the next cheap
plastic fake key looks just as seductive as the last
one did.

YANI 2: Schools, ready to gut. Various pocket quests
along the way down in the DoD, similar to Fort
Ludius, accessed by portals in locked closets (or
maybe vaults instead), such that if you don't wipe
out the school when you encounter the level,
thenceforth downward the Master *Smiths of those
schools make better locks, needing separate keys,
better, less detectable traps, more poisonous
poison, etc., or turn out graduate monsters better
able to evade traps, choose good weapons and armor,
read _appropriate_ scrolls, drink or throw
_appropriate_ potions, more skilled with weapons,
etc.

This might make a new conduct, too, "no schools
gutted".

Each pocket quest could be independently developed,
having them portal accessed makes that easier,
might have a "you hear students snoring" message
